  "review": {
    "verdict": "PASS",
    "feedback": "The statements are mathematically sound and consistent with the imported context. The extraction lemma explicitly assumes Q > 0 and r > 0; cube_prime_localization derives Q > 0 from Q | r^3 and r > 0, and derives i >= 1 from i = w*p^a + 2 with w >= 1, p prime, and a >= 1. The two cofactor identities are valid for all n, including the truncated n = 0,1 cases. The localization chain is accurate: Q ∤ r^2 together with Q | r^3 yields a prime q with q^3 | Q and q | r; the two reduced Q-factors are coprime, so q^3 transfers to i or i-1; and the separation lemma gives q = 2 or q coprime to w*p. The names do not overstate: cube_prime_localization asserts only existence of such a q and does not claim q^3 | Q or q | r in its conclusion, nor does it claim delta >= 13, gcd(delta,H) = 1, elimination of a delta-family, or the final ThirtyCofactor goal. Those are scope limitations, not defects in the stated theorems."
  },
